From 9c64c8cfd458d31ce6a59072ae85d935b9667493 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 22:39:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm, VideoRenderers: choose the frames for the
 measured time of their flips

In GBM the render manager chooses each frame for Kodi's fallback display
latency, the buffers plus one: three refresh periods. The ordinary output
and native Dolby Vision put a frame on the screen two periods after its
choice (the way that waits one, with a period taken off its latency), so
the picture leaves this computer a period before its sound, on every
driver. The logs of seven computers say so: -41.9 ms at 23.976 Hz on an RTX
4080 Laptop, an RX 550 and Intel Arrow Lake, -43.0 ms on a GTX 1060,
-17.1 ms at 60 Hz on Intel Raptor Lake. Where a new frame comes every second
or fifth refresh, Kodi presents each one a refresh sooner still.

For every frame chosen with the render manager's clock sync on, the window
system now measures its flip against the time its sound plays by that
latency alone (CMeasuredLatency: per way of presenting and display mode,
the interquartile mean and range of the last 2048 frames). Once at least 240
frames say clearly that it is a whole number of periods (the middle half
within a quarter of a period, the mean within a quarter of a period of a
whole number, no more than two periods sooner or one later), it is kept for
that GPU, driver and build, mode and way (the hidden setting
videoscreen.hadeslatency), and the render manager chooses the frames that
many periods later:
- only whole periods: the frames keep their offset from the refreshes, and
  only the refresh each one starts at moves, never which frames are shown
  or for how many refreshes;
- a new measurement is taken from the next video or seek on, never in the
  middle of what plays;
- without the clock sync (a refresh rate that is no whole multiple of the
  frame rate) nothing is measured or corrected;
- the latency tweak and the audio offset are left out of what is measured,
  and keep their meaning.
kodi.log says once a video what is taken and why, and what each video
measured. <measuredlatency>false</measuredlatency> under <video> in
advancedsettings.xml leaves Kodi's latency as it is (still measured and
told).

In a host model of the choice, in every case of those logs and with every
way of presenting, the picture against its sound went from a period before
it (two where frames come every second or fifth refresh) to within 2 ms
with "Sync playback to display", and to within half a period around it
without it, where the sound is left where it starts.

CorentinB pointed at the fixed estimate of the display latency, and at
measuring the time to the screen by the fence of the commit, in a pull
request to CroqueMr's build.
